### The Bus Speed Benchmark Sketch
Let's calculate and verify the massive transmission speed difference between I2C and SPI in Arduino C++.

Imagine sending a 1024-byte graphics buffer to a display:
- Over standard I2C (100 kHz, with address + ACK overhead): $\approx 92\text{ ms}$
- Over hardware SPI (8 MHz, zero framing overhead): $\approx 1.02\text{ ms}$
That is the difference between an unplayable 10 frames per second and an ultra-smooth 60+ FPS!

```cpp
// Monster Lab: Bus Speed Calculation Benchmark
void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);
  while (!Serial);
  Serial.println("=== BUS SPEED BENCHMARK: SPI vs. I2C ===");

  const unsigned long payloadBytes = 1024; // 1 KB display buffer

  // I2C 100 kHz: 9 clock pulses per byte (8 data bits + 1 ACK bit)
  float i2cClockSpeedHz = 100000.0;
  float i2cBitsPerByte = 9.0;
  float i2cTimeSec = (payloadBytes * i2cBitsPerByte) / i2cClockSpeedHz;

  // SPI 8 MHz: 8 clock pulses per byte (pure push-pull, no ACK overhead)
  float spiClockSpeedHz = 8000000.0;
  float spiBitsPerByte = 8.0;
  float spiTimeSec = (payloadBytes * spiBitsPerByte) / spiClockSpeedHz;

  Serial.print("Payload size: ");
  Serial.print(payloadBytes);
  Serial.println(" bytes");

  Serial.print("I2C 100kHz Transfer Time: ");
  Serial.print(i2cTimeSec * 1000.0);
  Serial.println(" ms");

  Serial.print("SPI 8MHz Transfer Time:   ");
  Serial.print(spiTimeSec * 1000.0);
  Serial.println(" ms");

  float speedup = i2cTimeSec / spiTimeSec;
  Serial.print("SPI is ");
  Serial.print(speedup);
  Serial.println("x faster for this buffer!");
}

void loop() {
  // Run once in setup
}
```

### Code Breakdown:
- `i2cBitsPerByte = 9.0`: Every byte on I2C requires a 9th clock tick for the peripheral's ACK bit.
- `spiBitsPerByte = 8.0`: SPI transmits exactly 8 clock pulses per byte with zero protocol framing waste.
- Notice the speedup: SPI completes the 1 KB transfer in approximately 1 millisecond, freeing the CPU to calculate navigation math!
